# ============================================================================
# TURING AND TAU - seeing that something cannot be satisfied, and WHY
# ============================================================================
#
# A program is run. It halts or it does not; a test passes or it fails. What
# a failing test tells you is: THIS input, THIS run, went wrong. What it can
# never tell you is that NO input, NO run could ever succeed - and since Rice
# (1953) that is not a weakness of your test suite, it is a theorem: no
# nontrivial property of a Turing-complete program is decidable. So in the
# Turing world "unsatisfiable" is not a verdict you can obtain; you observe
# failures one at a time, and you repair by guessing where the fault lives.
#
# Tau trades Turing completeness for decidability. A specification is a
# VALUE in an atomless Boolean algebra, and two questions have exact answers:
#   sat   S     is there ANY run that satisfies S?        (F = a proof of "no run")
#   valid S     does S hold on EVERY run?                 (T = a theorem)
# And when the answer is F, the engine has done the work of finding out WHAT
# clashes: the normalized remainder it prints along the way names the values
# a rule set FORCES, and the sub-questions you can pose isolate the one
# clause that closes the last door. That is the difference this file is
# about: a verdict with a reason, instead of a failure with a stack trace -
# and a repair aimed at the reason, instead of a repair by trial.
#
# Six parts: two questions Turing cannot ask (0), unsatisfiability as a proof
# and its control (1), the remainder as the WHY (2), finding the culprit by
# asking smaller questions (3), a repair that is targeted, then re-proved
# against the intent (4), and the case that motivated this file: a rule
# about YESTERDAY that could never bite, until the reason was named (5).
#
# Run with (minimal cost):
#   tau -q < turing_and_tau.tau
# Verified against 0.7.0-alpha, build 1c1e58ae (2026-09-02 main).
#
# READING THE OUTPUT: "%N: ..." lines answer the sat/valid queries with T or
# F - consecutive IDENTICAL results share one %N slot (the REPL's memory
# de-duplicates against the last entry), so match the answers by ORDER, not
# by number (annotations say "Answer:"). Each `sat always` query ALSO prints
# a line "Temporal normalization ... yielding the result: <formula>" - that
# formula is the REMAINDER, the part of the specification the engine could
# not settle; it is the "why" this file keeps pointing at. Remainders are
# normal forms and may differ in spelling between builds; the T/F lines are
# the machine contract.
# ============================================================================

# EXPECTED-RESULTS: T F F T T F F T F T T F T F F F T T

set charvar off

# ============================================================================
# PART 0: TWO QUESTIONS A PROGRAM CANNOT ANSWER ABOUT ITSELF
# ============================================================================
#
# Streams are named time series (o1, o2, ...); `o1[t]=1` says "o1 holds at
# every step"; `->` is implication, `&&` conjunction. A rule set is a
# sentence; `sat always` asks whether any run obeys it, `valid always`
# whether every run does. Both are decided, not tested.

sat always ((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1))
# Answer: T - a chain of two implications has runs (all zeros is one). A
# program would need an input to show you one run; the engine states that
# runs EXIST, for the whole (infinite) space of inputs at once.

valid always ((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1))
# Answer: F - and the same two rules are not a law of nature: some run
# violates them (o1=1, o2=0). "Not valid" is a proof that a counterexample
# exists - it is not the report of one failing test.

# ============================================================================
# PART 1: UNSATISFIABLE IS A PROOF, NOT A FAILURE
# ============================================================================
#
# Add a fact that contradicts the chain. The verdict is about the WHOLE set:
# there is no run at all, for any input, ever.

sat always ((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && o1[t]=1 && o3[t]=0)
# Answer: F - no run satisfies "o1 forces o3 through o2, and o1 holds, and
# o3 does not". A test suite could only show you that its inputs fail; this
# is the statement that success is impossible.

sat always ((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && o1[t]=1)
# Answer: T - the control: drop the clashing fact and runs exist again. Two
# queries, and the fault is bracketed between them - without running a
# single input.

# ============================================================================
# PART 2: THE REMAINDER IS THE WHY
# ============================================================================
#
# Look at the "yielding the result:" line the engine prints for the control
# query above. It reads
#   o1[t]:tau' = 0 && o2[t]:tau' = 0 && o3[t]:tau' = 0
# The apostrophe is Boolean complement, so `x' = 0` means "x is 1": the
# rule set FORCES o1, o2 and o3 to hold. The engine did not enumerate runs;
# it normalized the sentence, and the normal form says which values are no
# longer free. Reading that line is reading the reason - and reading it
# BEFORE adding the fact tells you in advance that o3=0 cannot be added.

sat always ((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && o3[t]=0)
# Answer: T - pin the OTHER end instead: with o3=0 the remainder names the
# escape: o3 = 0 && o1' != 0 && o2' != 0, i.e. "o1 and o2 must not be 1" -
# the only way out is that o1 never holds. (Note the exact wording: "not 1",
# not "0" - in an atomless algebra there is room between the two, and the
# engine says precisely what it knows, no more.) It tells you not just that
# a run exists, but what shape it must have.

sat always ((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && o3[t]=0 && o2[t]=1)
# Answer: F - and closing that escape (o2 must hold) closes the last door.
# Compare with part 1: two different facts kill the same chain, and in each
# case the remainder of the query WITHOUT the fatal fact named what the fact
# would have to contradict.

# ============================================================================
# PART 3: THE CULPRIT - ASK SMALLER QUESTIONS
# ============================================================================
#
# When a big rule set is unsatisfiable, the engine can also tell you WHICH
# clause is load-bearing: drop clauses one at a time and ask again. A clause
# whose removal turns F into T is a culprit; one whose removal changes
# nothing is innocent. Here the innocent bystander is o4.

sat always ((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && (o4[t]=1 -> o5[t]=1) && o1[t]=1 && o3[t]=0)
# Answer: F - four rules, one fact, no run.

sat always ((o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && (o4[t]=1 -> o5[t]=1) && o1[t]=1 && o3[t]=0)
# Answer: T - without the first link (o1 -> o2) the set is satisfiable: that
# link is a culprit.

sat always ((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && o1[t]=1 && o3[t]=0)
# Answer: F - without the o4 rule it is still unsatisfiable: o4 -> o5 is
# innocent. Three questions, no debugger, and the fault is localized to a
# clause. (A tau-nomic institution does exactly this when it explains a
# rejected proposal - "these two clauses conflict" - see part 08 of the
# series.)

# ============================================================================
# PART 4: A TARGETED REPAIR - AND ITS PROOF
# ============================================================================
#
# Knowing the culprit, repair it and nothing else. Three repairs are on the
# table: drop the fact (not a repair, a surrender), drop the rule (too much),
# or WEAKEN the rule with a condition so that the case at hand is an
# exception. Every candidate is one query; the one that is both satisfiable
# AND still enforces the original intent elsewhere is the repair.

sat always (((o1[t]=1 && o6[t]=0) -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && o1[t]=1 && o3[t]=0)
# Answer: T - "o1 forces o2, unless o6" (o6 marks the exception): with the
# exception available, the contradictory fact fits. The remainder shows
# o6 != 0 && o2' != 0 - the run must take the exception, and o2 must not be
# 1. The repair is visible in the reason, not only in the verdict.

valid always ((((o1[t]=1 && o6[t]=0) -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && o6[t]=0) -> (o1[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1))
# Answer: T - and the repaired rule still does its old job whenever the
# exception is NOT taken: outside o6, o1 still forces o3, on every run. This
# is the step Turing cannot offer: the repair is not "the failing test now
# passes", it is a theorem about all the runs you did not test.

valid always ((((o1[t]=1 && o6[t]=0) -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1)) -> (o1[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1))
# Answer: F - the honest control: WITHOUT restricting to the non-exception
# case, the old guarantee no longer holds for every run (the exceptional
# runs are exactly the ones where it fails). A repair has a price, and the
# price is a decided sentence, not a hunch.

# ============================================================================
# PART 5: THE RULE ABOUT YESTERDAY - a reason nobody would have tested for
# ============================================================================
#
# The case that motivated this file. `o12[t-1]` is "o12 one step ago". Write
# the rule "if o12 held yesterday, o13 is forbidden today", state the fact
# "o12 does not hold today", and ask whether o13 is allowed today.

sat always ((o12[t-1]=1 -> o13[t]=0) && o12[t]=0 && o13[t]=1)
# Answer: T - allowed. Now READ the remainder line: it ends in
# o12[t-1]:tau' != 0, i.e. "o12 one step ago is not 1". The engine is telling you
# that yesterday is FREE - nothing in the sentence pins it, so a run simply
# chooses a yesterday in which o12 did not hold, and the rule never bites.
# In the Turing world this rule would just run; you would write a test with
# a history, it would pass or fail, and no failure would ever say "your rule
# has no past".

sat always ((o12[t-1]=1 -> o13[t]=0) && o12[t]=1 && o13[t]=1)
# Answer: F - and with "o12 holds today" the rule DOES bite - because under
# `always` a fact about today is a fact about every step, yesterday
# included. The rule reads the present as if it were the past. Two verdicts,
# and the diagnosis is complete: the past has no value of its own.

sat always (o12[t]=0 && o12[t-1]=1)
# Answer: F - the proof that you cannot fix this by adding a fact: "today 0,
# yesterday 1" is unsatisfiable under `always`, for the same reason.

# The targeted repair, aimed at exactly what the remainder named: give the
# past a stream of ITS OWN. Read `o12[t-1]` as a separate stream (call it
# o9012) and pin THAT to what actually happened yesterday. Now the rule has
# a past, today keeps its own value, and the two verdicts come out the way a
# human means them:

sat always ((o9012[t]=1 -> o13[t]=0) && o12[t]=0 && o9012[t]=1 && o13[t]=1)
# Answer: F - yesterday o12 held (o9012=1), today it does not (o12=0), and
# o13 is now forbidden: the rule finally bites on the past, not the present.

sat always ((o9012[t]=1 -> o13[t]=0) && o12[t]=0 && o9012[t]=0 && o13[t]=1)
# Answer: T - yesterday it did not hold, so today is free. Same rule, same
# today, different yesterday: the verdict follows the past, as it should.

sat always ((o9012[t]=1 -> o13[t]=0) && o12[t]=0 && o13[t]=1)
# Answer: T - and without a pinned yesterday the past is free again - which
# is now an honest "unknown", not a silent "same as today".

# ============================================================================
# WHERE THIS LEADS
# ============================================================================
#
# Every step above was a question with a decided answer. Unsatisfiable was a
# theorem, the remainder was the reason, smaller questions found the culprit,
# the repair was aimed at it, and a validity check proved the repair kept
# the original intent everywhere else. None of these is available for a
# program: you can run it, test it, and read its trace, but you cannot ask
# it whether ANY run exists, or WHY none does. That is not a tooling gap; it
# is Rice's theorem. Tau pays for these questions with Turing completeness -
# and the tau-nomic series (parts 01-11) is what you can build once you have
# them: a constitution whose every amendment is admitted or refused by a
# proof, with the reason attached.
q
